    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having a nightmare with our Jenkins pipeline. We're seeing some really weird flaky tests in the unit test stage of our CI/CD. It's completely non-deterministic—the build will pass fine one minute, then fail the next without any actual changes to the source code or the configuration files. The error logs are super inconsistent; sometimes it's a timeout, other times it's a weird dependency resolution error that doesn't make sense. Any idea how I can start isolating the environmental variables or container state to figure out what's causing these intermittent failures?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To isolate environmental issues, refactor your Jenkins pipeline to always start with a fresh environment by marking stages or steps as `no-cache`, ensuring containers build from the latest agent rather than reusing cached ones. Additionally, consider adding a specific test environment with isolated dependencies and run each unit test in its own container instance to prevent shared state from causing flakiness. You can also enable Jenkins agent labels to manage resources properly and ensure consistent CPU/memory allocation, which often helps resolve intermittent timeout or dependency errors.",
