    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are right to suspect external interference; installing process monitoring tools like `dstat` or `iotop` temporarily on your Jenkins agents can help visualize CPU or I/O spikes that might indicate a rogue background task. Furthermore, wrapping your test execution in Docker containers with `.NET` or `Python` instrumentation can help isolate the process execution environment from the agent's file system. Finally, consider enabling \"Keep Logs\" but strictly limiting the retention period to force Jenkins to re-process failed builds from scratch, potentially excluding any cached artifacts that might be causing the interference.",
      "source": "target",
